Build succeeded. There's a new warning CS0162: unreachable code at AboutWindow.xaml.cs:21 — because `GitHubConfigured = true` is a `const`, the `if (!GitHubConfigured)` block is now dead code. The compiler sees a constant false, so the body is unreachable. Fix: change `const bool` to static readonly, or better — since GitHub is now configured, remove the dead-code path. The simplest way to keep the architecture: remove the `if (!GitHubConfigured)` block and the constant entirely (the no-repo notes are no longer applicable). But maybe I should keep the design pattern in case the repo gets removed? The user explicitly requested adding a link. The cleanest thing is to remove the dead branch and the constant.

Line 21 is inside `if (!GitHubConfigured) { ... }` = `GitHubNoteText.Text = "No GitHub repository has been configured..."`. Wait, line 21 is: let me check. Lines of the file:

```
16:    public AboutWindow()
17:    {
18:        InitializeComponent();
19:        VersionText.Text = $"Version {AppVersion}";
20:
21:        if (!GitHubConfigured)
22:        {
23:            GitHubButton.IsEnabled = false;
24:            GitHubNoteText.Text = "No GitHub repository has been configured for this project yet.";
25:        }
26:    }
```

So, let's simplify: remove the constant `GitHubConfigured` and the dead if-block. The GitHubNoteText element will remain (harmless, empty). No problem.

